# Never bake per-machine governance state or secrets into the image. These are provided at
# runtime via a mounted volume (Core pointer, operator registration, safety-flag activation,
# control state, ledger) and environment variables (bot token, API key). The Safety-Flag Gate
# depends on this: an image must never carry an activation that could enable a network call.
.runtime_governance_state/
THOMAS_CORE/approvals/
THOMAS_CORE/activations/
THOMAS_CORE/deactivations/
THOMAS_CORE/revocations/
THOMAS_CORE/CURRENT_CORE_RELEASE.yaml
# `.env*`, not `.env`. The exact-name pattern matched the live file and nothing beside it, so a
# BACKUP of it — `.env.bak-pre-605`, left in the repo root on 2026-08-08 by a session about to
# edit the real one — sat in the build context fully readable. That file is a copy of the venue
# API key, the model provider keys and the bot token. Nothing had baked it in yet only because
# the builds that ran happened to come from a clean worktree; a `docker compose build` from the
# repo root would have shipped it. Same widening for the credential extensions, which had the
# same hole: `key.bak` was not `*.key`.
#
# Deliberately wider than the observed miss. The failure mode here is that a secret file gets a
# suffix — an editor's `~`, a `.bak`, a dated copy — and the pattern that named the original
# stops matching it. No tracked file matches any of these, so widening costs nothing.
**/.env*
**/*.pem*
**/*.key*
**/*.p12*

# Dev cruft and anything the runtime service does not read.
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.venv/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.claude/
tests/
docs/
historical/
deferred/
generated/
examples/
programs/
tools/
.github/
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
requirements-validation.in
requirements-validation.lock
